s number six how shocking  was it to see Pinocchio Die For the  First Time Pinocchio visits the  underworld and speaks with death several  times throughout the film you cannot  truly truly die Guillermo del Toro has  said that he intended for death to serve  as the most motherly figure in  Pinocchio's life pointing out that  there's something very nurturing in the  way that death slowly guides Pinocchio  toward making the right decision Del  Toro went on to explain that he sees a  certain Beauty in the idea of death  especially as he gets older it's  beautiful because if  something doesn't end they've never be  gone number seven this movie doesn't  include the most frightening moment from  the Disney version help help somebody  help and yet what it replaces it with is  so much darker instead of being taken to  Pleasure Island Guillermo del Toro's  version sees Pinocchio recruited into  the village military Camp because the  fascist official in the town thinks if  this puppet cannot die and you will be  the perfect Soldier fun and games have  been replaced with drills and paintball  and rather than being turned into  donkeys these children are being turned  into soldiers Guillermo del Toro  admitted in an interview with Vanity  Fair that all this is very intense but  explained for me this is for both  children and adults that talk to each  other it tackles very deep ideas about  what makes us human number eight the  song is a far cry from When You Wish  Upon a Star I thought it would be  interesting to start the movie with  characters that sing much of the music  for Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio was  performed using wind instruments for  more chilling effect but by far the most  haunting song would have to be ciao Papa  performed by Gregory Mann Del Toro  described it as both the most moving  song in the film and the most important  song before going on to explain that it  talks about longing it talks about the  loss of a father and the loss of a son  composer Alexander Des flat spoke about  how he wanted ciao Papa to stand out as  its own unique moment stating I think  the lyrics say everything number nine  Pinocchio's relationship with Geppetto  is a bit more complicated this time  Guillermo del Toro described Geppetto by  saying he's a father that's very  concerned with the way everybody looks  at him and how Pinocchio makes him look  no no please it's a purpose this  combined with Pinocchio's more unruly  Behavior means that there's a lot more  conflict between them than there was in  the Disney animated classic you tell him  whom I love him and I won't be a burden  anymore but also over the course of the  story the one that one translation in  this Pinocchio is this guy Guillermo del  Toro says that this time around the  battle learns to be a real father as  opposed to pinocular learning to be a  real boy number 10. this theme of  Fathers and Sons appears in ways you may  not have noticed one scene in Guillermo  del Toro's version has Pinocchio being  forced to perform for Benito Mussolini  which ends with the puppet publicly  insulting him when asked why he chose to  include this scene Del Toro went on to  explain how he wanted to explore the  theme of Fathers and Sons in multiple  ways and that Mussolini represents a  more harmful version of fatherhood on  the other hand the film also includes  references to God and Jesus with  Pinocchio playing the role of surrogate  imperfect Messiah resurrecting and dying  for those that he loves number 11.  there's value and Disobedience although  Guillermo del Toro grew up loving Walt  Disney's version of Pinocchio he  admitted that there was one part of the  movie that always bugged him idea of  obedience and being something you're not  in order to be accepted he admitted that  one of his own personal challenges when  he set out to write the film was to see  can I make a Pinocchio that celebrates  Disobedience because of this breaking  the rules actually comes in handy a few  times throughout the film like when  Pinocchio has to escape from the belly  of the terrible Dogfish by lying number  12. beware the terrible Dogfish if you  think you know the story of Pinocchio  being that he eventually finds Geppetto  in the belly of a giant whale that was  actually a change made for the Disney  version for Guillermo del Toro's version  I'm going back to the original book and  bringing back the terrible Dogfish Carlo  collodi's novel described the Beast as  being larger than a five-story building  what are your thoughts on this darker  adaptation of Pinocchio do you think  they were an improvement or do you still  prefer the Disney version make sure to  let us know down in the comments and  don't forget to like And subscribe to  the things

